#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:1
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --install` - add packages to user environment"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:5
msgid "Synopsis"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:7
msgid ""
"`nix-env` {`--install` | `-i`} _args…_ \\[{`--prebuilt-only` | `-b`}\\] "
"\\[{`--attr` | `-A`}\\] \\[`--from-expression`\\] \\[`-E`\\] \\[`--from-"
"profile` _path_\\] \\[`--preserve-installed` | `-P`\\] \\[`--remove-all` | `-"
"r`\\] \\[`--priority` _priority_\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:16
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:18
msgid ""
"The `--install` operation creates a new user environment. It is based on the "
"current generation of the active [profile](../../command-ref/files/profiles."
"md), to which a set of [store paths](../../store/store-path.md) described by "
"_args_ is added."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:23
msgid "The arguments _args_ map to store paths in a number of possible ways:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:25
msgid ""
"By default, _args_ is a set of names denoting derivations in the [default "
"Nix expression](../../command-ref/files/default-nix-expression.md). These "
"are [realised](../../glossary.md#gloss-realise), and the resulting output "
"paths are installed. Currently installed derivations with a name equal to "
"the name of a derivation being added are removed unless the option `--"
"preserve-installed` is specified."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:33
msgid ""
"If there are multiple derivations matching a name in _args_ that have the "
"same name (e.g., `gcc-3.3.6` and `gcc-4.1.1`), then the derivation with the "
"highest _priority_ is used. A derivation can define a priority by declaring "
"the `meta.priority` attribute. This attribute should be a number, with a "
"higher value denoting a lower priority. The default priority is `5`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:40
msgid ""
"If there are multiple matching derivations with the same priority, then the "
"derivation with the highest version will be installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:43
msgid ""
"You can force the installation of multiple derivations with the same name by "
"being specific about the versions. For instance, `nix-env --install "
"gcc-3.3.6 gcc-4.1.1` will install both version of GCC (and will probably "
"cause a user environment conflict!)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:48
msgid ""
"If [`--attr`](#opt-attr) / `-A` is specified, the arguments are _attribute "
"paths_ that select attributes from the [default Nix expression](../../"
"command-ref/files/default-nix-expression.md). This is faster than using "
"derivation names and unambiguous. Show the attribute paths of available "
"packages with [`nix-env --query`](./query.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:56
msgid ""
"If `--from-profile` _path_ is given, _args_ is a set of names denoting "
"installed [store paths](../../store/store-path.md) in the profile _path_. "
"This is an easy way to copy user environment elements from one profile to "
"another."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:61
msgid ""
"If `--from-expression` is given, _args_ are [Nix language functions](../../"
"language/syntax.md#functions) that are called with the [default Nix "
"expression](../../command-ref/files/default-nix-expression.md) as their "
"single argument. The derivations returned by those function calls are "
"installed. This allows derivations to be specified in an unambiguous way, "
"which is necessary if there are multiple derivations with the same name."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:65
msgid ""
"If `--priority` _priority_ is given, the priority of the derivations being "
"installed is set to _priority_. This can be used to override the priority of "
"the derivations being installed. This is useful if _args_ are [store paths]"
"(../../store/store-path.md), which don't have any priority information."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:69
msgid ""
"If _args_ are [store paths](../../store/store-path.md) that point to [store "
"derivations](../../glossary.md#gloss-store-derivation), then those store "
"derivations are [realised](../../glossary.md#gloss-realise), and the "
"resulting output paths are installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:71
msgid ""
"If _args_ are [store paths](../../store/store-path.md) that do not point to "
"store derivations, then these are [realised](../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"realise) and installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:73
msgid ""
"By default all [outputs](../../language/derivations.md#attr-outputs) are "
"installed for each [store derivation](../../glossary.md#gloss-store-"
"derivation). This can be overridden by adding a `meta.outputsToInstall` "
"attribute on the derivation listing a subset of the output names."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:76
msgid "Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:78
msgid ""
"The file `example.nix` defines a derivation with two outputs `foo` and "
"`bar`, each containing a file."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:80
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"# example.nix\n"
"let\n"
"  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {};\n"
"  command = ''\n"
"    ${pkgs.coreutils}/bin/mkdir -p $foo $bar\n"
"    echo foo > $foo/foo-file\n"
"    echo bar > $bar/bar-file\n"
"  '';\n"
"in\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  name = \"example\";\n"
"  builder = \"${pkgs.bash}/bin/bash\";\n"
"  args = [ \"-c\" command ];\n"
"  outputs = [ \"foo\" \"bar\" ];\n"
"  system = builtins.currentSystem;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:99
msgid ""
"Installing from this Nix expression will make files from both outputs appear "
"in the current profile."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:110
msgid ""
"Adding `meta.outputsToInstall` to that derivation will make `nix-env` only "
"install files from the specified outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:112
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"# example-outputs.nix\n"
"import ./example.nix // { meta.outputsToInstall = [ \"bar\" ]; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:127
#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:151
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:129
msgid "`--prebuilt-only` / `-b`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:131
msgid ""
"Use only derivations for which a substitute is registered, i.e., there is a "
"pre-built binary available that can be downloaded in lieu of building the "
"derivation. Thus, no packages will be built from source."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:136
msgid "`--preserve-installed` / `-P`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:138
msgid ""
"Do not remove derivations with a name matching one of the derivations being "
"installed. Usually, trying to have two versions of the same package "
"installed in the same generation of a profile will lead to an error in "
"building the generation, due to file name clashes between the two versions. "
"However, this is not the case for all packages."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:145
msgid "`--remove-all` / `-r`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:147
msgid ""
"Remove all previously installed packages first. This is equivalent to "
"running `nix-env --uninstall '.*'` first, except that everything happens in "
"a single transaction."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:153
msgid ""
"The following options are allowed for all `nix-env` operations, but may not "
"always have an effect."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:155
msgid "`--file` / `-f` _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:157
msgid ""
"Specifies the Nix expression (designated below as the _active Nix "
"expression_) used by the `--install`, `--upgrade`, and `--query --available` "
"operations to obtain derivations. The default is `~/.nix-defexpr`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:162
msgid ""
"If the argument starts with `http://` or `https://`, it is interpreted as "
"the URL of a tarball that will be downloaded and unpacked to a temporary "
"location. The tarball must include a single top-level directory containing "
"at least a file named `default.nix`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:167
msgid "`--profile` / `-p` _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:169
msgid ""
"Specifies the profile to be used by those operations that operate on a "
"profile (designated below as the _active profile_). A profile is a sequence "
"of user environments called _generations_, one of which is the _current "
"generation_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:174
msgid "`--dry-run`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:176
msgid ""
"For the `--install`, `--upgrade`, `--uninstall`, `--switch-generation`, `--"
"delete-generations` and `--rollback` operations, this flag will cause `nix-"
"env` to print what _would_ be done if this flag had not been specified, "
"without actually doing it."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:181
msgid ""
"`--dry-run` also prints out which paths will be [substituted](../../glossary."
"md) (i.e., downloaded) and which paths will be built from source (because no "
"substitute is available)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:185
msgid "`--system-filter` _system_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:187
msgid ""
"By default, operations such as `--query --available` show derivations "
"matching any platform. This option allows you to use derivations for the "
"specified platform _system_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:195
msgid "Common Options"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:197
msgid "Most Nix commands accept the following command-line options:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:199
msgid "<span id=\"opt-help\">[`--help`](#opt-help)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:201
msgid "Prints out a summary of the command syntax and exits."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:203
msgid "<span id=\"opt-version\">[`--version`](#opt-version)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:205
msgid "Prints out the Nix version number on standard output and exits."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:207
msgid ""
"For machine-readable output, including versions of the libraries Nix links "
"against, see the [`nix version`](../../command-ref/new-cli/nix3-version.md) "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:210
msgid "<span id=\"opt-verbose\">[`--verbose`](#opt-verbose)</span> / `-v`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:212
msgid ""
"Increases the level of verbosity of diagnostic messages printed on standard "
"error. For each Nix operation, the information printed on standard output is "
"well-defined; any diagnostic information is printed on standard error, never "
"on standard output."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:216
msgid ""
"This option may be specified repeatedly. Currently, the following verbosity "
"levels exist:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:219
msgid "`0` “Errors only”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:221
msgid "Only print messages explaining why the Nix invocation failed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:223
msgid "`1` “Informational”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:225
msgid "Print _useful_ messages about what Nix is doing. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:228
msgid "`2` “Talkative”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:230
msgid "Print more informational messages."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:232
msgid "`3` “Chatty”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:234
msgid "Print even more informational messages."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:236
msgid "`4` “Debug”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:238
msgid "Print debug information."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:240
msgid "`5` “Vomit”"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:242
msgid "Print vast amounts of debug information."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:244
msgid "<span id=\"opt-quiet\">[`--quiet`](#opt-quiet)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:246
msgid ""
"Decreases the level of verbosity of diagnostic messages printed on standard "
"error. This is the inverse option to `-v` / `--verbose`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:249
msgid ""
"This option may be specified repeatedly. See the previous verbosity levels "
"list."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:252
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-log-format\">[`--log-format`](#opt-log-format)</span> _format_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:254
msgid ""
"This option can be used to change the output of the log format, with "
"_format_ being one of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:256
msgid "`raw`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:258
msgid "This is the raw format, as outputted by nix-build."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:260
msgid "`internal-json`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:262
msgid "Outputs the logs in a structured manner."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:264
msgid "**Warning**"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:266
msgid ""
"While the schema itself is relatively stable, the format of the error-"
"messages (namely of the `msg`\\-field) can change between releases."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:270
msgid "`bar`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:272
msgid "Only display a progress bar during the builds."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:274
msgid "`bar-with-logs`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:276
msgid "Display the raw logs, with the progress bar at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:278
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-no-build-output\">[`--no-build-output`](#opt-no-build-"
"output)</span> / `-Q`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:280
msgid ""
"By default, output written by builders to standard output and standard error "
"is echoed to the Nix command's standard error. This option suppresses this "
"behaviour. Note that the builder's standard output and error are always "
"written to a log file in `prefix/nix/var/log/nix`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:284
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-max-jobs\">[`--max-jobs`](#opt-max-jobs)</span> / `-j` "
"_number_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:286
msgid ""
"Sets the maximum number of build jobs that Nix will perform in parallel to "
"the specified number. Specify `auto` to use the number of CPUs in the "
"system. The default is specified by the `max-jobs` configuration setting, "
"which itself defaults to `1`. A higher value is useful on SMP systems or to "
"exploit I/O latency."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:291
msgid ""
"Setting it to `0` disallows building on the local machine, which is useful "
"when you want builds to happen only on remote builders."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:293
msgid "<span id=\"opt-cores\">[`--cores`](#opt-cores)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:295
msgid ""
"Sets the value of the `NIX_BUILD_CORES` environment variable in the "
"invocation of builders. Builders can use this variable at their discretion "
"to control the maximum amount of parallelism. For instance, in Nixpkgs, if "
"the derivation attribute `enableParallelBuilding` is set to `true`, the "
"builder passes the `-jN` flag to GNU Make. It defaults to the value of the "
"`cores` configuration setting, if set, or `1` otherwise. The value `0` means "
"that the builder should use all available CPU cores in the system."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:301
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-max-silent-time\">[`--max-silent-time`](#opt-max-silent-"
"time)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:303
msgid ""
"Sets the maximum number of seconds that a builder can go without producing "
"any data on standard output or standard error. The default is specified by "
"the `max-silent-time` configuration setting. `0` means no time-out."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:307
msgid "<span id=\"opt-timeout\">[`--timeout`](#opt-timeout)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:309
msgid ""
"Sets the maximum number of seconds that a builder can run. The default is "
"specified by the `timeout` configuration setting. `0` means no timeout."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:313
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-keep-going\">[`--keep-going`](#opt-keep-going)</span> / `-k`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:315
msgid ""
"Keep going in case of failed builds, to the greatest extent possible. That "
"is, if building an input of some derivation fails, Nix will still build the "
"other inputs, but not the derivation itself. Without this option, Nix stops "
"if any build fails (except for builds of substitutes), possibly killing "
"builds in progress (in case of parallel or distributed builds)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:319
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-keep-failed\">[`--keep-failed`](#opt-keep-failed)</span> / `-"
"K`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:321
msgid ""
"Specifies that in case of a build failure, the temporary directory (usually "
"in `/tmp`) in which the build takes place should not be deleted. The path of "
"the build directory is printed as an informational message."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:324
msgid "<span id=\"opt-fallback\">[`--fallback`](#opt-fallback)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:326
msgid ""
"Whenever Nix attempts to build a derivation for which substitutes are known "
"for each output path, but realising the output paths through the substitutes "
"fails, fall back on building the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:328
msgid ""
"The most common scenario in which this is useful is when we have registered "
"substitutes in order to perform binary distribution from, say, a network "
"repository. If the repository is down, the realisation of the derivation "
"will fail. When this option is specified, Nix will build the derivation "
"instead. Thus, installation from binaries falls back on installation from "
"source. This option is not the default since it is generally not desirable "
"for a transient failure in obtaining the substitutes to lead to a full build "
"from source (with the related consumption of resources)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:334
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-readonly-mode\">[`--readonly-mode`](#opt-readonly-mode)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:336
msgid ""
"When this option is used, no attempt is made to open the Nix database. Most "
"Nix operations do need database access, so those operations will fail."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:339
msgid "<span id=\"opt-arg\">[`--arg`](#opt-arg)</span> _name_ _value_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:341
msgid ""
"This option is accepted by `nix-env`, `nix-instantiate`, `nix-shell` and "
"`nix-build`. When evaluating Nix expressions, the expression evaluator will "
"automatically try to call functions that it encounters. It can automatically "
"call functions for which every argument has a [default value](../../language/"
"syntax.md#functions) (e.g., `{ argName ?  defaultValue }: ...`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:345
msgid ""
"With `--arg`, you can also call functions that have arguments without a "
"default value (or override a default value). That is, if the evaluator "
"encounters a function with an argument named _name_, it will call it with "
"value _value_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:348
msgid ""
"For instance, the top-level `default.nix` in Nixpkgs is actually a function:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:357
msgid ""
"So if you call this Nix expression (e.g., when you do `nix-env --install --"
"attr pkgname`), the function will be called automatically using the value "
"[`builtins.currentSystem`](../../language/builtins.md) for the `system` "
"argument. You can override this using `--arg`, e.g., `nix-env --install --"
"attr pkgname --arg system \\\"i686-freebsd\\\"`. (Note that since the "
"argument is a Nix string literal, you have to escape the quotes.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:361
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-arg-from-file\">[`--arg-from-file`](#opt-arg-from-file)</"
"span> _name_ _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:363
msgid ""
"Pass the contents of file _path_ as the argument _name_ to Nix functions."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:365
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-arg-from-stdin\">[`--arg-from-stdin`](#opt-arg-from-stdin)</"
"span> _name_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:367
msgid "Pass the contents of stdin as the argument _name_ to Nix functions."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:369
msgid "<span id=\"opt-argstr\">[`--argstr`](#opt-argstr)</span> _name_ _value_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:371
msgid ""
"This option is like `--arg`, only the value is not a Nix expression but a "
"string. So instead of `--arg system \\\"i686-linux\\\"` (the outer quotes "
"are to keep the shell happy) you can say `--argstr system i686-linux`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:374
msgid "<span id=\"opt-attr\">[`--attr`](#opt-attr)</span> / `-A` _attrPath_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:376
msgid ""
"Select an attribute from the top-level Nix expression being evaluated. (`nix-"
"env`, `nix-instantiate`, `nix-build` and `nix-shell` only.) The _attribute "
"path_ _attrPath_ is a sequence of attribute names separated by dots. For "
"instance, given a top-level Nix expression _e_, the attribute path `xorg."
"xorgserver` would cause the expression `e.xorg.xorgserver` to be used. See "
"[`nix-env --install`](../../command-ref/nix-env/install.md) for some "
"concrete examples."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:382
msgid ""
"In addition to attribute names, you can also specify array indices. For "
"instance, the attribute path `foo.3.bar` selects the `bar` attribute of the "
"fourth element of the array in the `foo` attribute of the top-level "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:387
msgid ""
"<span id=\"opt-eval-store\">[`--eval-store`](#opt-eval-store)</span> _store-"
"url_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:389
msgid ""
"The [URL to the Nix store](../../store/types/index.md#store-url-format) to "
"use for evaluation, i.e. where to store derivations (`.drv` files) and "
"inputs referenced by them."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:391
msgid "<span id=\"opt-expr\">[`--expr`](#opt-expr)</span> / `-E`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/install.md:393
msgid ""
"Interpret the command line arguments as a list of Nix expressions to be "
"parsed and evaluated, rather than as a list of file names of Nix "
"expressions. (`nix-instantiate`, `nix-build` and `nix-shell` only.)"
msgstr ""

